{#- Follows infloww.com/download: one section per surface, then theirs for comparison. -#}
<p>
  <strong>There is no app to download.</strong> Someone installs Inflowwww on a server once; then everyone opens it in
  a browser, on a computer or a phone.
</p>
<p><strong>Setup and updates mean typing a few commands on a server. After that, everyone on the team works in a browser.</strong></p>
<p>{% include "tos.njk" %}</p>

<h2>Web: your server's URL</h2>
<p>
  The web app is the download. Run the <a href="{{ '/help/' | url }}">Quick start</a>, then forward the port from your
  own computer (<code>ssh -L 3001:127.0.0.1:3001 user@server</code>) and open
  <code>http://127.0.0.1:3001/?t=&lt;SHARE_TOKEN&gt;</code>; see <a href="{{ '/help/#first-login' | url }}">First login</a>.
  The token is remembered after the first visit. From any other machine, use the server's URL: the domain you put in front of port 3001 with a reverse
  proxy, or the <code>tunnel</code> profile's Cloudflare URL (see <a href="{{ '/help/' | url }}">Help</a>).
</p>

<h2 id="capture">Capture a session</h2>
{% include "capture-flow.njk" %}
<p>
  The creator's account will be seen from the server's IP (or the proxy you assign) from then on; assign a proxy before
  pasting if that matters.
</p>

<h2>Phone: the same web app</h2>
<p>
  Open your server's URL (not 127.0.0.1) on your phone. The layout is responsive: the sidebar becomes a drawer and
  Messages Pro shows one pane at a time. There is no native app in the App Store or on Google Play.
</p>
{% shot "messages-pro-thread-phone.webp", "A fan chat in Messages Pro on a phone: a photo, a paid PPV and a tip, from a demo agency" %}

<h2>Desktop: build the Electron shell</h2>
<p>
  Most teams can skip this: the web app in a browser is the same product. The desktop app lives in
  <code>desktop/</code>. It asks for your server URL on first launch and loads the web UI from there, so everyone signs
  in as they would in the browser. The desktop app's own capture button does not work against this relay today, and
  it does not capture Fansly sessions; use the Chrome extension.
</p>
<pre class="break-all whitespace-pre-wrap"><code>cd desktop
npm install
npm start          # run it
npm run dist:mac   # arm64 and x64 .dmg in desktop/dist/
npm run dist:win   # Windows .exe installer (builds on macOS too)</code></pre>
<p>
  Builds are unsigned. An app you build yourself opens normally. If you copy the .dmg to another Mac, macOS may say the
  app "is damaged and can't be opened"; that is the missing signature. The desktop README's only workaround is
  right-click &gt; Open the first time, and it says to sign and notarize the build before handing it to a
  non-technical team. On Windows, SmartScreen asks you to confirm (More info &gt; Run anyway). Details are in <a href="{{ site.repo }}/blob/main/desktop/README.md">desktop/README.md</a>.
</p>
{% shot "messages-pro.webp", "Inflowwww desktop window showing the Messages Pro inbox of a demo agency, with a paid PPV and a tip in the open thread" %}

<h2>Where are the binaries?</h2>
<p>
  We don't host any. There is no download server and no installer on this site: you build the app from source you can
  read, and git clone downloads it. The checkout page is a git clone.
</p>

<h2>Theirs, for comparison</h2>
<p>
  Infloww's download page offers a desktop app as Mac <code>.dmg</code> files for Apple Silicon and Intel, asks for
  macOS 12 or later, 8GB of RAM and 10GB of free storage, and has a "Looking for the Windows download?" toggle
  (infloww.com/download, page stamped Oct 10, 2026).
</p>
